  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nTurning out to be another beautiful Sunday afternoon across the \nnorthern plains today. Current temperatures reaching the low 50s \nacross western MN and upper 40s in western WI with only sparse \npockets of high clouds on satellite. Winds near 20 mph have been \nobserved across the Buffalo Ridge this afternoon which will \nonly spread east as we enter the first half of this week. Warm \nadvection will be stronger over western MN tonight which will \nkeep lows in the mid to upper 30s compared to western WI seeing \nmid-20s for a least one more night. \n\nThe forecast remains pretty much on track with the previous \ndiscussion. Broad high pressure will keep much of its grasp over the \neastern half of the CONUS. However, a slight jaunt eastward will aid \nin the low-level flow over the northern plains to increase over for \nthe first half of this week. Therefore leaving Monday through \nWednesday dry and abnormally warm. Ensemble guidance continues to \nadvertise surface temperature anomalies ranging from 10-20 degrees \nwarmer than normal values (low 40s/upper 20s). Skies during this \ntime will range from mostly sunny to partly cloudy and highs in the \n50s. Conditions will be breezy though given the shift into increased \nsoutherly flow. Winds gusts will range between 20-30mph each day. \nAlthough we are looking to be warm, it looks like historical daily \nrecords for the next three days will go undisturbed but we could \nsneak into the top 10 daily warmest temperatures. Especially if we \nfactor in our overnight temperatures creeping into the mid to upper-\n40s in eastern MN into western WI.\n\nThe second half of the week features at least two potentials for \nprecip. The first being a weak wave that arrives Wednesday night but \nguidance continues to advertise this feature as mainly moisture \nstarved. Latest NBM guidance with this system favors the more \nprevalent PoPs across northern Minnesota however areas east of I-35 \ninto western WI could wake up to a couple hundredths of \nrainfall by sunrise Thursday morning. The more robust system \narrives late Friday into the weekend. Upper-level ridging that \nhad been dominant earlier in the week gives way to a troughing \npattern over the western CONUS. The exact track remains unknown \nhowever most solutions favor a warm- sector scenario. As this \nsystem passes, cold advection wrapping around the back side \ncould cool the vertical profile enough to produce a few \nsnowflakes.",
